---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { solutions } from '../data/solutions.ts';

const stackOrder = ['gsm', 'sie', 'itip', 'saf'];
const stack = stackOrder
  .map((slug) => solutions.find((s) => s.slug === slug))
  .filter((s) => s !== undefined);

const stackRoles: Record<string, string> = {
  gsm: 'The standard',
  sie: 'The engine',
  itip: 'The first domain application',
  saf: 'The agentic bridge',
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Poesis is a systems company: we author the GSM standard, engineer the SIE engine and ITIP application that run it, bridge them to agentic AI with SAF, and deliver the transformation around them."
  pageType="About"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="motto">Define the world in software; generate it.</p>
      <h1>A systems company for the generative era.</h1>
      <p class="lead">Poesis authors a standard, engineers the stack that implements it, and runs its own operation on both. GSM defines how systems are described and governed; SIE evaluates and realizes those definitions; ITIP applies them to IT; SAF puts agentic AI to work inside them. Around the stack, our services carry organizations through the transformation it enables.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">The thesis</p>
      <h2>Definitions belong in software, not in prose.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Every organization already runs on definitions — architectures, obligations, processes, constraints. Today they live in documents that drift from reality the day they are written, and every governance activity downstream inherits that drift: audits reconstruct, reviews guess, automation acts blind.</p>
      <p class="lead">Our thesis is that these definitions should be typed, versioned, machine-operable objects — evaluated continuously against the real world, realized automatically, and always subject to human disposition. Generative AI makes this tractable for the first time; systemic governance makes it safe. We call the result <strong>governed synthetic autopoiesis</strong>: systems that continuously produce and renew their own organization without escaping the definitions, constraints, and human purposes that govern them.</p>
      <p class="lead">The theory is not ours to invent — it is systemics. GSM synthesizes Beer&rsquo;s Viable System Model, von Bertalanffy&rsquo;s General System Theory, Wiener and Ashby&rsquo;s cybernetics, and Maturana and Varela&rsquo;s autopoiesis into a single generative definition of systems. Our contribution is making that lineage executable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we build</p>
        <h2>One thesis, four layers.</h2>
        <p class="lead">Each layer feeds the next, closing the Think → Build → Run loop — the standard defines, the engine realizes, the domain application applies, the agentic framework delivers.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        {stack.map((s) => (
          <article class="card card--feature card--static">
            <span class="tag">{stackRoles[s.slug]}</span>
            <h3>{s.name} — {s.fullName}</h3>
            <p>{s.description}</p>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm service-link" href={s.href}><Icon name="arrow-right" /> Explore {s.name}</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.6rem">The stack is generalist by design. IT is the first domain — where the definitions, constraints, and feedback loops are most exposed — with <a href="/research/">Research</a> emerging as the second. Around it, <a href="/#services">four service lines</a> take organizations from transformation strategy to sovereign deployment, each with an explicit boundary of ownership, risk, and support agreed before any work begins.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--ink">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">How we operate</p>
      <h2>We hold ourselves to the standard we ship.</h2>
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div class="proof-item">
          <span class="k">Poesis runs on Poesis</span>
          <p>We are our own first custody. Every Poesis repository — engine, applications, these sites — is defined and governed as GSM Definitions on our own platform, measured by the same 29 appraisal indicators we ship.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof-item">
          <span class="k">Humans dispose</span>
          <p>Agents and mechanisms propose; humans decide. Every consequential action in the stack passes a human gate — autonomy is earned inside explicit definitions, never assumed outside them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof-item">
          <span class="k">Open by construction</span>
          <p>The GSM specification is published source-available (BUSL-1.1), developed in the open, and prepared for neutral stewardship — standards live longest when no single product owns them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="proof-item">
          <span class="k">Evidence over prose</span>
          <p>Sourced frameworks cite their clauses, contributions carry provenance envelopes, compliance is computed from the model — every claim the stack makes is traceable to its source, and so are ours.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Leadership</p>
      <h2>Founded by practitioners, not observers.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Poesis was founded on a thesis its founders had already lived: careers spent governing, building, and selling enterprise systems — and watching the definitions those systems depend on decay in documents. The company exists to close that gap.</p>
      <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="founder-head">
            <img class="founder-avatar" src="/clement-cazaud.jpg" alt="Portrait of Clément Cazaud" width="72" height="72" loading="lazy" />
            <div>
              <span class="tag">Founder</span>
              <h3>Clément Cazaud</h3>
              <p class="title">Chief Executive, Product &amp; Architecture Officer</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Enterprise and solutions architect, software engineer, agentic AI expert, and systems thinker. Clément has spent his career turning sprawling IT landscapes into coherent, governed systems — mapping architectures, leading transformations, and building the tooling that makes governance executable rather than aspirational. GSM and the Poesis stack are the generalization of that work, made tractable by the agentic AI era.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/clement-cazaud" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clément on LinkedIn →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="founder-head">
            <img class="founder-avatar" src="/brahim-ben-helal.jpg" alt="Portrait of Brahim Ben Helal" width="72" height="72" loading="lazy" />
            <div>
              <span class="tag">Co-Founder</span>
              <h3>Brahim Ben Helal</h3>
              <p class="title">Chief Growth Officer</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>A career at the intersection of enterprise software and commercial strategy — SaaS, AI, digital trust, and enterprise sales. Brahim designs the go-to-market systems that turn complex platforms into adopted ones. At Poesis he leads growth, ecosystem development, and customer engagement — keeping the platform accountable to real enterprise problems, not just to its own architecture.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/company/poesis-cloud" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poesis on LinkedIn →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="founder-head">
            <img class="founder-avatar" src="/hamza-abidi.jpg" alt="Portrait of Hamza Abidi" width="72" height="72" loading="lazy" />
            <div>
              <span class="tag">Co-Founder</span>
              <h3>Hamza Abidi</h3>
              <p class="title">Chief Technology &amp; Engineering Officer</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Software engineer and architect with a track record on large-scale systems where performance, security, and long-term maintainability are non-negotiable — Enedis, ING through BlueSoft, the French Football Federation. At Poesis, Hamza owns the technical vision and platform engineering: the foundations the stack scales and evolves on, from the engine to the applications built above it.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:.6rem"><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/abidihamza" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hamza on LinkedIn →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cta" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact"><Icon name="message" /> Talk to us</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/poesis-cloud"><Icon name="github" /> See the work on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .founder-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .founder-avatar {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 72px;
    height: 72px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    object-fit: cover;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(45, 212, 191, .12);
  }
  .founder-head .tag + h3 {
    margin-top: .5rem;
  }
</style>

